{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/consensus-dao-jing-v2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tSession-ID: 01a0779a-7a9d-79e0-930a-ac19af7164b4 (captured from thread.started)\n     2\t\n     3\tI’ll read the docket and repository evidence, then rule on each contested item independently. I won’t edit files or run tests.\n     4\tThe host-boundary wording is stronger than Decision 2’s intake-pool argument: it explicitly rules out function-shaped host adapters. I’m also checking whether the reviews distinguish a raw remote storage API from materialization, and temporary v1 coexistence from a promise of dependency isolation.\n     5\tMy tentative rulings uphold the host-boundary objection and the RPC lifecycle omissions. I’m narrowing C2: the dependency table is inaccurate, but the cited migration rules don’t clearly require transitive isolation at J2. For N1, I favor coordinated removal when the replacement lands; its ownership and test destination depend on the C1 decision.\n     6\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-06 16:45:07 GMT\n     7\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-06 23:45:07 Asia/Bangkok\n     8\tCoding-Agent: codex\n     9\tSession-ID: 01a0779a-7a9d-79e0-930a-ac19af7164b4\n    10\t\n    11\tReferences below use `plan` for `collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`, `brief` for its `.prompt.md` counterpart, and `rpc` for `src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc`. Settled docket facts are accepted. No files were edited and no tests were run.\n    12\t\n    13\t**C1 | POSITION: uphold finding |** Sol is right about the architectural conflict. The decisive text is not merely Axiom 1: `docs/design/datom.world.md:53–68` explicitly makes a host boundary a stream boundary, sanctions DaoStream operations as its function-shaped contact, and rejects callable host adapters as substitutes. Decision 2 removes that contact and places filesystem operations behind the content-handle functions (`plan:205–216`).\n    14\t\n    15\tGLM’s reasoning fails because `docs/design/dao.jing.md:278–282` specifies the **content-handle interface**, not permission for arbitrary host IO beneath it. Indeed, that same document specifies the file implementation as an append-only **log stream** at lines 297–303. Explicit backend functions and an underlying stream are therefore compatible; the former does not exclude the latter. The intake establishes the publication boundary, but does not establish that every subsequent filesystem operation is outside the host-boundary rule. Reader count is irrelevant to that rule. Storage ignorance concerns content semantics (`dao.jing.md:185–200`), not a prohibition on reusable byte framing.\n    16\t\n    17\t**Plan change:** Replace Decision 2 with a conforming v2 durable-log dependency and its conformance obligations, or obtain an explicit superior-design amendment permitting this particular backend arrangement before J1. A v2 implementation must specify durability completion; merely renaming synchronous filesystem wrappers would not meet the no-wait contract (`dao.stream.md:153–158`). This is a design prerequisite, not a mechanical five-call replacement.\n    18\t\n    19\t**C2 | POSITION: partial, specified — uphold inaccurate dependency claims; reject mandatory J2 extraction |** Sol correctly identifies a real transitive dependency. `plan:64–66,408` deliberately requires `dao.jing`; its settled v1 require means Decision 4’s “none” entries are inaccurate as dependency claims. The table even counts a transitive dependency for `dao.jing.coordinate` (`plan:312–320`), so silently interpreting every other row as direct-only is inconsistent.\n    20\t\n    21\tHowever, Sol has not established that J2 **must** be transitively v1-free. The brief permits a justified parallel migration (`brief:90–95`), and the stream migration explicitly allows temporary coexistence with deletion after the last consumer migrates (`docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:642–659`). A v2 observer calling shared materialization code does not thereby operate on v1 readers.\n    22\t\n    23\tGLM is closer on the remedy: an explicitly temporary dependency can be acceptable. But it is not *inherent* to fold-back—extraction would avoid it—and one sentence attached only to the end condition would leave the table and J0 misleading.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Plan change:** Retain the single shared implementation for this migration, but distinguish direct dependencies from transitive legacy dependencies throughout Decision 4, J0, J2 and the end condition. State that pre-J5 deployment still needs v1, and make J5 remove the legacy dependency from the migrated observer/remote dependency closure. Extract a shared core before J2 only if independent v2 deployment is an actual requirement; the cited evidence does not currently impose it.\n    26\t\n    27\t**C3 | POSITION: partial, specified — uphold missing materialization replacement; reject equating raw backend put with materialize! |** The proposed client does not preserve the complete remote materialization operation. `plan:264–289` exposes address-supplied put and raw verdicts; the required derivation and read-back verification belong to materialization (`docs/design/dao.jing.md:160–166`; settled `src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:249–296`). No stepped equivalent is specified.\n    28\t\n    29\tBut `request-put` is not intrinsically defective for being a backend primitive. The existing backend interface also receives an address and returns a verdict; `materialize!` supplies the higher-level guarantee. Likewise, existing remote handlers validate the incoming address and invoke raw put (`src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:46–68`). Backend and materializer are distinct layers.\n    30\t\n    31\tThe consequential gap is that the old client could be composed with `materialize!`, while the new one explicitly cannot (`plan:235–242`). Calling this the completed async backend dependency at `plan:304–308,568–570` leaves responsibility for replacing that integrity-preserving composition unstated. Read-only hydration can use asynchronous get without materialization; write-side readiness is a stronger claim.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Plan change:** Keep J3’s raw content RPC adapter, explicitly defer a caller-stepped materializer to a named DaoJing follow-on phase, and qualify the backend-readiness claim. Require the follow-on to derive the address, put, conditionally get on `:present`, reject absent/unequal read-back, and complete with the address only after verification. `store-tree-async` must depend on that operation; read hydration need not.\n    34\t\n    35\t**C4 | POSITION: uphold finding |** Sol is right. The brief expressly says not to leave its four questions to a phase that “will decide” (`brief:52–56`), and question four includes the final naming decision (`brief:90–95`). Yet J5 says to take a decision and offers a recommendation (`plan:504–510`).\n    36\t\n    37\tGLM correctly observes that the stream plan postpones its own naming choice (`docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:655–659`). That precedent does not override this brief’s explicit requirement. A recommendation is not a settled decision.\n    38\t\n    39\t**Plan change:** Decide now: fold the observer into `dao.jing` and rename `dao.jing.v2.remote` to `dao.jing.remote` once their v1 consumers have migrated. J5 executes that decision. Coordinate with a stream rename if convenient, but do not make that rename an additional prerequisite.\n    40\t\n    41\t**C5 | POSITION: uphold finding, with a qualification about retention |** GLM identifies an unresolved lifecycle obligation. The specified wrapper allocates an ID for `pending-request`, but gives neither its step nor its reattachment contract responsibility for retiring that unsent request (`plan:269–292,469–474`). The settled behavior of `lose-outstanding` and `rebind` means the ordinary outstanding-request loss path does not settle it.\n    42\t\n    43\t“Silently swallowed” is slightly imprecise: RPC retains the request, rather than discarding it. Retention across bindings is not inherently forbidden either. `rpc:267–276` explicitly assigns the decision to the composition. The defect is that this composition specifies neither deliberate retention nor abandonment while promising reconnection behavior. Fixing A1 alone does not settle that policy.\n    44\t\n    45\t**Plan change:** Choose abandonment for terminal bindings. After polling, if the returned RPC state is terminal and still has an unsent request, call `rpc/abandon-unsent` with that terminal reason before draining completions. Specify the same abandonment obligation before an explicit writer-binding change, using `:dao.stream.rpc/abandoned` when no terminal reason exists. Preserve monotonic IDs and publish the loss exactly once. Add unsent-at-detach and rebind cases proving the old envelope is never sent on the new attachment.\n    46\t\n    47\t**C6 | POSITION: uphold finding |** The enumeration is incomplete. Response-reader cursor defects become terminal loss reasons (`rpc:408–413`), while `plan:283–285` omits them. It also fails to distinguish reader `:dao.stream/end` from lifecycle `:dao.stream.apply/ended`. The settled allocator-error outcome is missing from `plan:271–274`.\n    48\t\n    49\tThese are reachable protocol results, not discretionary diagnostics. Conversely, totality need not mean exposing every internal RPC outcome: correctly constructed Jing operations always supply a keyword and vector, excluding RPC’s invalid-request branch (`rpc:246–250`).\n    50\t\n    51\t**Plan change:** Decode any RPC completion carrying a reason into `{:id … :op … :lost reason}`, preserving the fully qualified reason unchanged. Label examples as examples, not a closed vocabulary. Include `allocator-error` in request outcomes, specify that allocation failure creates no new request ID/completion, and retain its diagnostic. Cover cursor defects, reader end, allocation failure and abandonment in J3a.\n    52\t\n    53\t**N1 | POSITION: partial, specified — support prompt retirement and coverage transfer; reject unconditional Jing-owned deletion as written |** Accepting the docket’s consumer inventory, keeping an orphaned v1 log until unrelated v1 consumers migrate has no demonstrated production purpose. The copy at `plan:212–220` creates a needless second framing owner. Retirement should accompany the replacement.\n    54\t\n    55\tHowever, the exact proposed destination depends on C1. If a superior amendment authorizes private file framing, moving the recovery coverage into `file_test.cljc` is correct. Under my C1 ruling’s default route—a v2 log transport—the framing recovery tests belong with that transport, with Jing integration coverage proving successful recovery and subsequent durable materialization. Moving all framing responsibility into Jing tests would put the evidence away from its implementation owner.\n    56\t\n    57\tOn **jurisdiction**, use a named stream-owned dependency. The brief explicitly limits scope (`brief:103–108`), and the current plan promises v1 streams remain untouched (`plan:543–548`). The brief’s specific permission to own a *v2 append-log phase* (`brief:73–74`) does not clearly authorize deleting an existing v1 transport.\n    58\t\n    59\t**Plan change:** Add a proposed dependency named **“legacy append-log retirement”**, owned by the stream migration and coordinated atomically with J1’s replacement. It removes `dao.stream.log`, its obsolete suite and test-runner references after replacement coverage exists. Jing owns the integration evidence; the surviving framing owner owns torn-tail recovery evidence. Under an approved private-framing decision, that owner becomes Jing. Do not defer retirement to wholesale v1 deletion, and do not include the separate live-tail `dao.stream.file` transport.\n    60\t\n    61\t**What would change my mind**\n    62\t\n    63\t- **C1:** An accepted superior-design clause explicitly classifying content-store handles as sanctioned host interpreters, including how their outcomes satisfy the stream-emission rule, would support Decision 2. The existing “explicit functions” sentence does not supply that clause.\n    64\t- **C2:** A documented requirement that J2 load or deploy without legacy namespaces would make extraction mandatory. A concrete load-time interference caused by this dependency would also move it from documentation debt to an implementation blocker.\n    65\t- **C3:** A specified async composition that already owns address derivation and conditional read-back would remove the materialization gap. An explicit read-only meaning for “async backend” would justify the hydration claim, but would still require naming the separate write dependency.\n    66\t- **C5:** An explicit retained-request policy, with attachment-independent operation semantics and evidence that every pending ID eventually completes or can be abandoned, would justify replay across rebind instead of mandatory abandonment.\n    67\t- **N1:** Explicit orchestrator authorization extending Jing’s scope to this orphan’s retirement would move deletion ownership into J1. A named near-term production dependency on the old log would justify delaying deletion; its own remaining tests would not.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/consensus-dao-jing-v2-r2.notes.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Round 2 — orchestrator notes, verified against the tree\n     2\t\n     3\tFour facts neither round-1 position had, all checked. They are settled;\n     4\targue from them, do not re-derive them.\n     5\t\n     6\t**N-a. The no-wait rule cuts against building a v2 append-log.**\n     7\t`dao.stream.md:153-158` states it generally: \"no operation waits. An operation\n     8\tthat cannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n     9\tcalled.\" But `dao.jing.file` today acknowledges a put **only after the log is\n    10\tflushed** (`dao.jing.md:299-301`), and `dao.jing/materialize!` returns the\n    11\taddress synchronously only after the backend answers `:inserted`. So a\n    12\tconforming v2 append-log could not simply wrap synchronous file writes:\n    13\tdurability completion would have to arrive later, as data. gpt-6-astra already\n    14\tconceded this (\"merely renaming synchronous filesystem wrappers would not meet\n    15\tthe no-wait contract... a design prerequisite, not a mechanical five-call\n    16\treplacement\"). Both seats must now say what that does to C1: if option (a)\n    17\trequires redesigning when a content put is acknowledged, it is not a\n    18\tlike-for-like alternative to option (b), and its cost must be stated in those\n    19\tterms rather than as conformance-suite work.\n    20\t\n    21\t**N-b. `datom.world.md:66-68` says, verbatim:** \"An adapter that exposes a\n    22\tfunction for portable code to call is not an interpreter. It isolates the host\n    23\tlibrary but keeps the coupling, and the effect never appears as an emission.\"\n    24\t`:put-content-fn` is exactly such a function. claude-fable-5-1's C1 argument is\n    25\tthat this condemns DaoJing's content-handle map **today, on v1**, and would\n    26\tstill condemn it with a v2 append-log underneath — so the transport cures\n    27\tnothing the axiom actually objects to. gpt-6-astra must answer that argument\n    28\tdirectly: either the handle map is already outside Host Boundaries (in which\n    29\tcase C1 is not what fixes it), or explain what a v2 log beneath a synchronous\n    30\t`:put-content-fn` changes about the emission.\n    31\t\n    32\t**N-c. Correction to claude-fable-5-1's C2 count.** The v1 observer has **no\n    33\t`src/` caller at all** — confirmed, `observer-state`/`observe-step!` appear\n    34\tonly in tests. But the repoint touches **seven** test files, not five: the five\n    35\t`test/dao/space/` files (transactor, stigmergy, index, schema, query) plus\n    36\t`test/dao/jing/mem_test.cljc:213` and `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:402`, which\n    37\tare not `dao.space` tests and would not travel with `jing_test.cljc`. Nine\n    38\t`src/` namespaces require `dao.jing` (including `src/dev/psset_fixtures.clj`),\n    39\tnone for the observer.\n    40\t\n    41\t**N-d. Scope for N1 is the orchestrator's call, not yours.** gpt-6-astra's\n    42\tfalsifier was \"explicit orchestrator authorization extending Jing's scope\",\n    43\tand claude-fable-5-1 named \"leave `log.cljc` standing\" as its fallback if scope\n    44\tis held closed. That ruling has not been made. Rule **conditionally**: state\n    45\twhat should happen if a consumer plan may delete a v1 file, and what should\n    46\thappen if it may not. Do not assume either.\n    47\t\n    48\tAlso verified: `log_test.cljc:104` writes `(->bytes [11 22])`, which is not a\n    49\tdecodable `[address payload]` record — claude-fable-5-1's porting adaptation is\n    50\tcorrect. `yin.repl.driver` calls `rpc/abandon-unsent` at driver.cljc:220,\n    51\t258, 293, 305, including before every rebind.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-consensus.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/architect-dao-jing-v2-consensus.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-06 16:45:28 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-06 23:45:28 +07 (Asia/Bangkok)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: claude\n     4\tSession-ID: e425d8bd-ad4c-44f7-aaed-54cb3196fd0f\n     5\t\n     6\t# Consensus round 1 — author seat positions\n     7\t\n     8\tOne fact I did not have when drafting, checked now and load-bearing below: the v1 observer (`dao.jing/observer-state`, `observe-step!`) has **no consumer under `src/`**. Every caller is a test: `test/dao/jing_test.cljc`, `test/dao/jing/{file,mem,dht}_test.cljc`, and five files under `test/dao/space/` (`index`, `query`, `schema`, `transactor`, `stigmergy`). The `dao.stream` require at `jing.cljc:19` exists solely for `ds/next` at `jing.cljc:391`, which only tests reach. That reshapes C2 and C4.\n     9\t\n    10\t---\n    11\t\n    12\t**C1 | POSITION: reject finding (Sol); uphold plan, with two concessions** | Sol's strongest text is `datom.world.md:51-59` — \"A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call\" and \"DaoStream handle operations are the one sanctioned function-shaped contact with that boundary.\" Under v1 the file write went through a sanctioned handle op (`ds/append!`, `file.cljc:149`); under the plan it goes through raw host calls. That reading fails for a specific reason: **the coupling Sol objects to is above the log, not below it, and option (a) does not remove it.** Portable code reaches the file backend through `:put-content-fn`, a synchronous function returning `:inserted`/`:present` (`jing.cljc:277`), and `materialize!` returns the address synchronously. Whether a v1 log stream, a v2 append-log, or a raw `RandomAccessFile` sits *beneath* that function is invisible to every caller — `dao.data.btree.storage` and `dao.space.index` call `jing/materialize!` and `jing/get` and nothing else. If the handle map violates Host Boundaries (\"an adapter that exposes a function for portable code to call is not an interpreter\", `datom.world.md:66-68`), it violates it today, on v1, and would still violate it with a v2 append-log underneath. Building the transport buys conformance evidence for a stream nobody observes and cures nothing Sol names. GLM's reading is the correct one: the intake pool is DaoJing's stream boundary; `dao.jing.md:278-282` already commits to \"backend effects are explicit functions, not a protocol or hidden state\"; and the backend effect is the far side of interpretation — Datomic's storage service is a KV, not a log, in exactly this position. Sol's \"reader count does not determine whether IO is a stream\" is true and beside the point: the argument was never that one reader makes it not-a-stream, but that a stream whose only reader is its only writer, with no cursor ever handed out and no descriptor ever projected, uses none of the contract's guarantees — so the contract's evidence obligations are being paid for nothing. \"`dao.jing.mem` performs no IO so it is not precedent\" misreads the precedent: it is precedent for *where the backend effect lives*, not for what kind of effect it is. Two concessions Sol earns: (i) `dao.jing.md:297-299` currently *says* \"backed by an append-only log stream\", so the plan is changing a design-doc commitment, not merely code — J4 already rewrites that line, but Decision 2 must say explicitly that it amends `dao.jing.md`'s *Implemented surface*, not `datom.world.md`; (ii) the pre-existing question of whether DaoJing's synchronous handle map conforms to Host Boundaries is real and belongs in `dao.jing.md`'s open items, routed there by name, not silently. | **Change:** add both sentences to Decision 2; add the handle-map/Host-Boundaries question to J4's open-items edit. Expected? Yes — item 1 on my contested list, and it split the reviewers exactly as predicted.\n    13\t\n    14\t**C2 | POSITION: partial — uphold the defect, reject the fix shape** | Sol is right that Decision 4's \"v1 dependency after this plan: none\" for `dao.jing.v2` is false as written and that the J2 gate must be transitive; GLM's \"clarifying sentence\" leaves a false isolation claim standing with a footnote, which the runtime plan's own R0 discipline would not accept. But Sol's fix — extract a stream-free core and make `dao.jing` a composition over it — inverts the diff: it touches every one of the eight `src/` requirers of `dao.jing` (`btree/storage`, `space/index`, `space/schema`, `jing/{file,mem,remote,dht,dht.node}`) to relocate code that is *already* stream-free, in order to isolate two functions that **nothing in `src/` calls**. The offending dependency is `jing.cljc:19`, held alive by `jing.cljc:391` alone, reached only by tests. The minimal correct fix is the other direction: **move the v1 observer out**, into a v1 namespace `dao.jing.observer` (unchanged code, unchanged `dao.stream` require), and repoint the five `test/dao/space/` files' `jing/observer-state` → `observer/observer-state` (one require line and an alias per file). `dao.jing` then requires no `dao.stream` from J1 onward, the transitive claim becomes true and testable, and J5's deletion is \"delete `dao.jing.observer`\" rather than surgery inside `dao.jing`. This touches five test files outside `dao.jing*` — a scope broadening the brief forbade — so I state it as the recommendation with the fallback being GLM's sentence plus an honest \"transitively v1 until J5\" in Decision 4 and the end condition. | **Change:** J1 gains \"move v1 observer to `dao.jing.observer`, repoint five space tests\"; Decision 4 table row for `dao.jing` becomes \"v1 dependency: none (observer moved out)\"; J2 gate reads \"no namespace under `dao.jing.v2*` requires `dao.stream` **directly or transitively**\". Expected? Partly — item 3 on my list anticipated a challenge to the observer-only `dao.jing.v2`, but on duplication grounds; the transitive-dependency angle I missed, and I missed it because I never checked who calls the v1 observer.\n    15\t\n    16\t**C3 | POSITION: uphold finding** | Sol is right and I have no rescue. `materialize!` is not \"put\": it derives the address (`jing.cljc:276`), and on `:present` reads back and throws on inequality or absence (`jing.cljc:281-293`), pinned by `jing_test.cljc:240-263`. My `request-put address payload` hands the client a caller-supplied address and returns raw `:inserted`/`:present` — that is v1 `content-client`'s `:put-content-fn` (`remote.cljc:92-94`), i.e. the backend effect, and v1 got its integrity behavior only because `jing/materialize!` wrapped it. Removing the handle removed the wrapper and I did not replace it, then called the result \"the async DaoJing backend\" that `dao.data.btree.md` §5.4 waits for — which that section defines as `hydrate-async`/`store-tree-async` returning a Promise/Future (`btree.md:853-863`), a driver composition, not a state machine. Both claims were wrong. | **Change:** replace `request-put` with **`request-materialize state payload`**: derives the address locally, issues `:jing/put-content`; on `:inserted` completes `{:op :materialize :address a :result :inserted}`; on `:present` issues `:jing/get-content` for the same address as a second correlated request, and completes `:present` only when the returned value equals the payload, else an `:error` with `:dao.jing.v2.remote/integrity-failure` or `/present-but-absent` — as data, because inside a driver step an exception has nowhere to go. The client holds a per-id `{:payload :address :phase}` record for the verify hop. Reclassify J3: it delivers stepped remote materialization and get; the promise-shaped \"async backend\" of §5.4 is a driver over it, deferred to `dao.space`'s plan with that dependency named. Expected? No — not on my list. I wrote \"`jing/materialize!` cannot be called against a remote store\" and stopped, without asking what does its job.\n    17\t\n    18\t**C4 | POSITION: uphold finding** | Sol is right on both counts: the brief asked for \"what the naming decision is at the end\" (`prompt.md:95`), and my own rule (plan §Decisions: \"settled here so no phase decides them alone\") forbids leaving it as a recommendation. Coupling DaoJing's fold-back to `dao.stream`'s eventual rename was an unforced dependency — DaoJing's names appear on no wire. GLM's \"mirrors the stream plan's end-condition language\" is true but that language was the stream plan's *own* deferral of *its* name; a consumer plan citing it as license to defer is precedent-borrowing, not reasoning. **Decided now, and made coherent with the C2 fix:** the end state is `dao.jing` (stream-free core), `dao.jing.observer` (the observer, on v2), `dao.jing.remote` (handlers plus stepped client). During coexistence the v2 pieces are `dao.jing.v2.observer` and `dao.jing.v2.remote`; J5 deletes v1 `dao.jing.observer` and v1 `dao.jing.remote`, then renames the two `dao.jing.v2.*` namespaces into those slots. `dao.jing.v2` as a bare namespace disappears from the plan; the `.v2.` segment is the transient marker and nothing else. Independent of whatever `dao.stream` decides. | **Change:** Decision 4 table and J5 rewritten accordingly. Expected? No — not on my list; I thought \"recommendation: fold back\" was settled enough. It was not.\n    19\t\n    20\t**C5 | POSITION: uphold finding** | GLM is right, verified per the docket: `lose-outstanding` reduces over `:outstanding` only (`rpc.cljc:308-315`); an envelope that returned `pending-request` is in `:unsent`, not `:outstanding` (`rpc.cljc:185-190` moves it only on `ok`); so on `/detached` it produces no completion, and `rebind` (`rpc.cljc:465-476`) carries it onto the new writer. My J3b claim \"killing the connection completes the outstanding request as `:lost`\" is true only for accepted requests. `rpc/abandon-unsent` (`rpc.cljc:267-283`) exists for exactly this and `yin.repl.driver` already uses it before every rebind and on operator disconnect (`driver.cljc:230-262`); I had read that driver and still did not carry the rule over. Combined with A1 this left a caller holding an id no operation could retire. | **Change:** (a) A1's fix shape — `step` first re-attempts an unsent envelope via `rpc/request!` (its unsent branch ignores op/args, `rpc.cljc:243-244`) and folds that outcome into the step result before polling; (b) when `step` observes a terminal transition — `/detached` included — with `:unsent` held, it calls `rpc/abandon-unsent` with the terminal reason, so the unsent request completes as `:lost` through the ordinary outbox; (c) the client exposes `abandon state reason` for the composition's own disconnect-before-rebind, mirroring the REPL driver; (d) J3a gains an unsent-at-detach case, J3b a kill-with-unsent case asserting exactly one `:lost` completion and an empty `:unsent` after rebind. Expected? No. Item 6 on my list anticipated a *preference* debate about `busy`; what landed was a deadlock (A1) plus this swallow, both from not tracing `rpc.cljc` past its docstrings.\n    21\t\n    22\t**C6 | POSITION: uphold finding** | Verified: `poll-read` reports `cursor-mismatch` and `invalid-cursor` as terminal loss reasons (`rpc.cljc:408-413`); `allocate-request` returns `allocator-error` on the first failure and sets terminal (`rpc.cljc:155-161`), so `request!` answers `terminal` only on subsequent calls (`rpc.cljc:240-241`). My enumerations were incomplete on both sides, and a decode written from them literally would fall through. The correct shape is not a longer list. | **Change:** completion decode is total by construction — a completion carrying `:dao.stream.rpc/response` decodes by op; one carrying `:dao.stream.rpc/reason` becomes `{:lost reason}` for **any** reason keyword, pass-through, including `:dao.stream.rpc/abandoned` from C5's fix; `request-*` outcomes are the RPC layer's outcome set verbatim, `allocator-error` included, with a declaration-driven test that every keyword the RPC namespace can emit has a branch. Expected? No.\n    23\t\n    24\t**N1 | POSITION: uphold the proposal; this plan claims the deletion** | On the merits: after J1, `dao.stream.log` (252 lines) has exactly one non-test consumer today (`file.cljc:190`) and none after; its `defopen :append-log` (`log.cljc:252`) is a load-time registration into the ambient registry v2 retires; and the copy-not-require choice leaves two owners of the truncation loops (`log.cljc:203-210, 222-230, 237-248`) until v1 dies — GLM's drift hazard. Deleting it in J1 removes the duplicate owner the moment it is created and lets A3's coverage travel with the code. **Jurisdiction:** this plan claims it, for three reasons. The orphaning is caused here, and leaving dead code for another plan to notice is the \"defect of the migration\" language in reverse. The stream plan's end condition deletes v1 \"when the last consumer has migrated (under its own plan)\" (`dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:655-657`) — for this transport the last consumer is `dao.jing.file`, migrating under this plan, so per-transport deletion at the consumer's migration is the stream plan's rule applied at its natural granularity, not a broadening. And handing it to the stream plan \"with a named dependency\" is strictly worse than leaving it standing: same dead code, plus a tracking obligation for an owner with no reason to act before wholesale deletion. The honest cost: it is the first v1 file deleted by a consumer plan, and the brief said not to broaden beyond `dao.jing*`. So the plan states it in *Boundary* as the one deletion outside `dao.jing*`, with the reason, and names \"leave `log.cljc` standing until v1 dies\" as the acceptable fallback if the orchestrator rules the scope closed. The port of `torn-tail-test` needs one adaptation the proposal does not mention: `log_test.cljc:104` writes raw bytes `[11 22]`, which is not a decodable `[address payload]` record, so `create-content-file` would fail closed on it before reaching the torn tail — the ported test must first append one valid encoded jing record, then hand-write the overlong-length frame, then assert truncation, replay of the valid record, and a clean subsequent put; plus the sub-four-byte-tail variant Sol asked for, on all three hosts. | **Change:** J1 deletes `src/cljc/dao/stream/log.cljc` and `test/dao/stream/log_test.cljc`, ports `torn-tail-test` with the adaptation above, and the *Boundary* section records the jurisdiction claim and fallback. Expected? No — neither reviewer saw it either.\n    25\t\n    26\t---\n    27\t\n    28\t## What would change my mind\n    29\t\n    30\t**C1 (least confident of the rejections).** Evidence that some *other* layer needs cursors over the content file — a durable observer checkpoint design that reads the content log positionally, or a `dao.space` recovery path that replays it — would make the log a stream with a second reader and flip me to option (a) or to re-basing on a future v2 log transport. Also: if the orchestrator reads `datom.world.md:66-68` as condemning DaoJing's handle map *and* wants that fixed now, then the right move is not a v2 append-log but a redesign of `materialize!` as an effect on a stream, which is a different and larger plan; I would concede C1 as \"blocked on that\" rather than \"build the transport\".\n    31\t\n    32\t**C2 (confident on the defect, less on the fix).** If the orchestrator holds the `dao.jing*` scope line absolutely, the five-test-file repoint is out and I fall back to GLM's sentence plus an honest transitive claim. What would make me abandon the move-out entirely: a `src/` consumer of the v1 observer I failed to find. My grep was `observer-state\\|observe-step!` over `src` with `--include='*.clj*'`; a caller reaching it through `ns-resolve` or a string would evade it, and I do not believe one exists.\n    33\t\n    34\t**C3.** Nothing would move me back to raw `request-put`. What could change the *shape*: if a reviewer shows the read-back-verify hop is unnecessary over RPC because `default-handlers` already validates address-against-payload server-side (`remote.cljc:57`) — then `:present` from a conforming server already implies equality, and the second hop guards only against a non-conforming server. I would still keep it, because `materialize!` keeps it locally against a conforming backend for the same reason, but I would accept it being declared optional per composition.\n    35\t\n    36\t**C4.** Only an argument that `dao.jing.observer` is the wrong final name — e.g. that the observer should fold into `dao.jing` proper because `observer-state` is DaoJing's entire operational surface. I chose the split because it is what the C2 fix produces for free and because it keeps the stream-facing code separable from the address-minting core, which C2 showed is worth having.\n    37\t\n    38\t**C5, C6.** Repository facts, verified by the orchestrator; nothing would move me. The only open question is the step's ordering — re-attempt unsent *before* or *after* polling — and I chose before, because a `/detached` observed in the poll would then abandon an envelope the same step had just re-attempted; a reviewer showing that ordering produces a duplicate completion would make me reverse it.\n    39\t\n    40\t**N1.** A ruling that consumer plans may not delete v1 files would end it; I would then choose \"leave standing\" over \"hand off\". Evidence that `dao.stream.log` has a consumer I missed — some `(require 'dao.stream.log)` for its `defopen` side effect in a demo or `src/dev` — would also end it; the docket says only three requirers exist and I did not re-derive that.\n    41\t\n    42\t**Scorecard against my own contested list.** Items 1 (Decision 2) and 6 (`busy`) were challenged, the first as expected and the second far harder than expected. Items 2, 4, 5 and 7 were not contested. Item 3 was contested on a ground I had not considered. Four findings I did not anticipate at all — C3, C4, C5, C6 — plus A2, A3 and N1, and every one of them came from the seam between what I specified and what `rpc.cljc` and `log.cljc` actually do. The pattern is plain: the decisions held, the protocol details did not, because I reasoned from the sibling plans' prose instead of the two source files.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
